New CSS:

```css
/* Working-hours rows: day, then two time groups (hour / minute / period) */
@media (max-width: 991px) {
  .availability-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    padding: var(--space-3);
  }

  .availability-row > .availability-day {
    flex: 1 1 calc(100% - 54px);
    width: auto;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* the delete control shares the first line with the day */
  .availability-row > .availability-remove-btn {
    order: 1;
    flex: 0 0 44px;
    height: 44px;
  }

  .availability-row > .availability-from-hour,
  ... {
    order: 2;
    flex: 1 1 calc(33.333% - 14px);
    width: auto;
    min-width: 0;
  }
  ...
}
```
Careful with `order`: default 0 for all; day order 0, remove order 1, time selects order 2, sep order 2. Then the line-breaking order is day, remove, sep, from×3, to×3 — but sep would come before the from selects! Because sep is order 2 as well and appears earlier in DOM (after from selects). DOM order within same order value: day(0), from×3(2), sep(2), to×3(2), remove(1). Sorting by order: day, remove(1), then from×3, sep, to×3 → correct! sep after from selects.

Set sep `order: 2; flex: 1 1 100%; text-align: center; font-size: var(--fs-sm)`.

Then line 1: day (100%-54px) + remove (44px) + gaps → 100% exactly if gap counted: day basis calc(100% - 54px) + 44 + gap 10 = 100% + ... Let me compute: container content width W. day basis W-54, remove 44, gap 10 → total = W-54+44+10 = W. Fits exactly. Good.

But `flex-grow: 1` on day would stretch it: `flex: 1 1 calc(100% - 54px)` means grow 1; after the two items fit exactly, no free space. But rounding could wrap. Safer: `flex: 1 1 auto; max-width: calc(100% - 54px)`? Hmm. Use `flex: 1 1 0; min-width: 0` for the day and `flex: 1 1 100%`? No...

Alternative simpler: make the day `flex: 1 1 calc(100% - 54px)` and remove `flex: 0 0 44px`, gap 10. In box-sizing terms gap is extra: line = day(100%-54) + gap(10) + 44 = 100% exactly. OK, exact fit works, but any border/padding rounding... The container's content box width W is what percentages resolve against. Fine. To be safe use calc(100% - 56px) so there's 2px slack.

Hmm, actually is putting the delete button next to the day the best UX? The delete deletes the whole day row, so being on the day line is logical and it stops it from dangling.

Alternatively keep the delete at the end of the row (last line) as it is now, after the to-group. In the screenshot it sits alone on a line, left-aligned — looks stray. On the day line it's compact.

I'll put it on the day line.

Now the time selects: `flex: 1 1 calc(33.333% - 14px)`. Three per line with 2 gaps (20px): total = 3*(33.333%W - 14) + 20 = W - 42 + 20 = W - 22 → fits with 22px slack, grows to fill. Good.

But minute vs hour vs period have very different content ("00" vs "صباحاً"). Equal thirds = 1/3 each; period text "صباحاً" at 16px ≈ 53px, column ≈ (W-20-20)/3 ≈ (358-40)/3 = 106 → plenty.

At 768: column ≈ (694-40)/3 = 218 each. Selects stretched wide — visually okay? A 218px "09" select is odd. Could cap: `max-width: 160px`? Then they'd be left-aligned in RTL and leave a gap. Hmm, use `justify-content: flex-start`? With flex-grow they fill. I could limit with `max-width: 140px` and `flex-grow: 0`? Then extra space at the end of the line.

Alternatively at 577-991 use 6 columns per line? At 768, 6*100 = 600 fits; but at 600, 6*90=540 fits... The current ≤768 code does exactly that (all 6 on one line) and it looks cramped at 393.

Compromise: two-column-per-line? no.

I'll accept stretched selects at tablet widths — they're readable and consistent. Actually, stretched selects at 768 look like the day select (which is also stretched). Fine, uniform.

Hmm, one more consideration: on mobile, should the "إلى" separator be centered on its own line? It reads fine. Cost: 1 row (~20px). Keep.

Now, the base (desktop) rules:
```
.availability-row select,
.availability-row input {
  font-size: var(--fs-md);
  padding-block: 8px;
  padding-inline: 10px 30px;
}
```
Wait `.form-select` sets `padding-left: 32px` — my `padding-inline: 10px 30px` in RTL → left = 30. Good, slightly tighter than 32. Actually simpler to keep 32: `padding-inline: 10px 32px`. Then text area in a 70px box = 70-32-10-2 = 26px. "09" at 14px ≈ 19px → fits. At 16px (tablets) 22px → fits barely. OK.

Hmm, but the hour options include "12" (2 digits) and "—", and the minute "00". 26px is tight but enough.

Desktop `.availability-row .availability-from-hour { width: 70px }` — at ≥992 font is 14px. Let me bump hour/minute to 74px for breathing room. The row total grows by 16px — fine.

Also `.form-select` has `background-position: left 12px center` — with padding-left 32, the chevron sits inside. Good, no overlap.

The screenshot showed chevrons overlapping because padding was 8px while the chevron is at 12px from the left. Fixing padding to 32px solves it.

Now, do I need `!important` removal to matter? `.availability-row select` (0,1,1) beats `.form-select` (0,1,0) and `.auth-form-control` (0,1,0) → yes, works without important. But `.form-select` is defined in design-system.css (loaded before site.css) anyway.

Also for ≥992 the `font-size: var(--fs-md)` = 14px — same as other inputs. Good.

What about `#adminAvailabilityContainer` duplicates (110/70/95 widths)? Keep them; they're the same values. But they set `flex-shrink: 0` — in the mobile stack, `flex-shrink: 0` with a width of 110 would prevent shrinking. My mobile rules must override: use `#adminAvailabilityContainer .availability-row > .availability-day { width: auto; min-width: 0; }` etc. Since I target with `> *` I need specificity ≥ (0,2,0) for those.

Simplest: in the media query, add a catch-all with high specificity:
